1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,474
This video shows how to build the three CANScope nodes, one connection at a time.

2
00:00:06,474 --> 00:00:12,407
Every picture is a schematic illustration generated from the project's pin ledger.

3
00:00:12,407 --> 00:00:19,415
It is not a photograph of your modules, so always read the labels printed on your own parts.

4
00:00:19,415 --> 00:00:28,344
Pause the video whenever you need to. Each step stays on screen until its narration ends.

5
00:00:28,344 --> 00:00:35,802
Node one is the motor ECU simulator. Node two is the battery ECU simulator.

6
00:00:35,802 --> 00:00:44,670
Node three is the CANScope monitor, with an OLED display, three status LEDs, two buttons and a buzzer.

7
00:00:44,670 --> 00:00:52,529
Each ESP32 talks to the bus through its own SN65HVD230 transceiver.

8
00:00:52,529 --> 00:01:00,922
The bus is one straight line: end A, Node one, Node two, Node three, end B.

9
00:01:00,922 --> 00:01:08,911
There is a 120 ohm terminator at each physical end, and nowhere else.

10
00:01:08,911 --> 00:01:11,890
Here is the honest status.

11
00:01:11,890 --> 00:01:18,204
No board, module or wire in this video has been physically inspected or measured.

12
00:01:18,204 --> 00:01:28,877
Most connections are provisional. You may make a provisional connection only after the bench checks, called gates, listed for it have passed.

13
00:01:28,877 --> 00:01:36,335
The buzzer transistor connections are blocked, because the transistor part number is not known yet.

14
00:01:36,335 --> 00:01:49,743
The current firmware only brings up CAN. It does not drive the OLED, LEDs, buttons or buzzer, and it does not simulate the motor or battery or inject faults yet.

15
00:01:49,743 --> 00:01:58,211
So this video builds and checks the hardware. It does not claim the peripherals work.

16
00:01:58,211 --> 00:02:09,779
Here is the inventory. Three ESP32 DevKit V1 thirty-pin boards and three SN65HVD230 transceiver modules.

17
00:02:09,779 --> 00:02:24,726
One SSD1306 I2C OLED, three LEDs, three 220 ohm resistors, two 120 ohm resistors, two buttons and one five volt active buzzer.

18
00:02:24,726 --> 00:02:30,255
Two breadboards, jumper wires and three USB data cables.

19
00:02:30,255 --> 00:02:41,127
You must add an NPN transistor and a one kilo-ohm base resistor. They are needed so that the buzzer is never driven directly by a GPIO pin.

20
00:02:41,127 --> 00:02:47,426
You also need a digital multimeter. Every gate in this video uses it.

21
00:02:47,426 --> 00:02:57,714
A ten kilo-ohm pull-down is recommended, and a 1N4148 diode is needed only if your buzzer is electromagnetic.

22
00:02:57,714 --> 00:03:07,132
Safety rules. Unplug every USB cable before you wire, measure resistance, or change anything.

23
00:03:07,132 --> 00:03:11,521
There is no external five volt supply in this build.

24
00:03:11,521 --> 00:03:19,929
Never connect CAN high or CAN low to an ESP32 pin. Only the transceiver touches the bus.

25
00:03:19,929 --> 00:03:27,887
Never put five volts on a three volt three pin, and never join the three volt three pins of two different nodes.

26
00:03:27,887 --> 00:03:32,461
Never drive the buzzer straight from a GPIO pin.

27
00:03:32,461 --> 00:03:39,100
This is a bench simulator. Never connect it to a vehicle or to a real network.

28
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:46,463
If a step is marked blocked, stop. Do not guess.

29
00:03:46,463 --> 00:03:54,647
Every wiring step shows a wire number, where it starts, where it ends, the signal and the status.

30
00:03:54,647 --> 00:04:01,300
Confirmed means a named document supports it. You still read the printed labels.

31
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:10,878
Provisional means you must first pass the listed gates. Gates are short bench checks, numbered G1 to G14.

32
00:04:10,878 --> 00:04:16,817
Blocked, shown as a dashed red line, means do not wire it.

33
00:04:16,817 --> 00:04:22,811
Write every gate result on the sign-off sheet in the manual.

34
00:04:22,811 --> 00:04:30,164
Before any wire, flash each bare board, with nothing connected except its USB cable.

35
00:04:30,164 --> 00:04:38,117
A new board may already run unknown firmware that drives random pins, which could fight the transceiver later.

36
00:04:38,117 --> 00:04:47,816
Node one gets the node1 firmware. It listens in normal mode and acknowledges frames, but does not transmit.

37
00:04:47,816 --> 00:04:57,209
Node two gets the legacy test sketch. It is the only firmware that transmits, sending fixed test frames.

38
00:04:57,209 --> 00:05:02,377
Node three gets the monitor firmware, which is listen-only.

39
00:05:02,377 --> 00:05:13,665
All three use GPIO seventeen for CAN transmit and GPIO sixteen for receive, at 500 kilobits per second, matching the ledger.

40
00:05:13,665 --> 00:05:20,469
Unplug the board, then label it Node one, two or three.

41
00:05:20,469 --> 00:05:27,292
Hold each ESP32 with the components up and the USB connector pointing left.

42
00:05:27,292 --> 00:05:42,945
Gate G1: read the marking on the metal shield. It must say ESP-WROOM-32, not WROVER. On a WROVER, GPIO sixteen and seventeen are used by memory and cannot be CAN pins.

43
00:05:42,945 --> 00:05:51,728
Gate G2: read all thirty pin labels. Pin one of each header is the pin next to the USB connector.

44
00:05:51,728 --> 00:05:59,157
The top row in these diagrams is the VIN side. The bottom row is the 3V3 side.

45
00:05:59,157 --> 00:06:07,220
If your labels are in a different order, stop and send a photo. Do not count pins from the drawing.

46
00:06:07,220 --> 00:06:17,568
Pins shaded dark red are flash, strapping or USB serial pins. CANScope never uses them.

47
00:06:17,568 --> 00:06:25,571
This is the SN65HVD230 transceiver, marked VP230 on the chip.

48
00:06:25,571 --> 00:06:40,643
Gate G4: check the chip marking, and check that the module's supply pin is labelled 3V3 or VCC with no five volt marking. The chip runs from three point zero to three point six volts.

49
00:06:40,643 --> 00:06:54,326
CTX, also called TXD or D, is the transmit input. It connects to the ESP32 transmit pin. This is not crossed like a UART.

50
00:06:54,326 --> 00:07:04,915
CRX, also called RXD or R, is the receive output. It connects to the ESP32 receive pin.

51
00:07:04,915 --> 00:07:17,642
Gate G5 checks the Rs mode pin. It must be pulled to ground for high-speed mode. If it is high, the chip is in standby and cannot transmit or acknowledge.

52
00:07:17,642 --> 00:07:28,546
If your module exposes a pin labelled RS, S or STB, wire it to ground. Leave Vref unconnected.

53
00:07:28,546 --> 00:07:36,988
Gate G6: many of these modules already have a 120 ohm resistor between CAN high and CAN low.

54
00:07:36,988 --> 00:07:42,987
With the module unpowered and connected to nothing, measure CAN high to CAN low.

55
00:07:42,987 --> 00:07:51,335
Above ten kilo-ohms means no onboard terminator. About 120 ohms means there is one.

56
00:07:51,335 --> 00:07:58,029
If the Node one or Node three module has one, leave out that end's external resistor.

57
00:07:58,029 --> 00:08:07,177
If the Node two module has one, stop. That resistor must be removed, so send a photo first.

58
00:08:07,177 --> 00:08:13,121
The aim is exactly two terminators on the whole bus.

59
00:08:13,121 --> 00:08:28,869
Gate G7: read the four OLED labels. G N D, V C C, S C L, S D A is common, but some modules swap ground and V C C. Always connect by the printed name.

60
00:08:28,869 --> 00:08:36,842
The OLED is powered from three volt three only, so its pull-ups keep the data lines at three volt three.

61
00:08:36,842 --> 00:08:50,760
Gate G9: the LED's long leg is the anode. The short leg, next to the flat edge, is the cathode. Confirm with the meter's diode test.

62
00:08:50,760 --> 00:09:00,343
Gate G8: on a four-leg button, two diagonally opposite legs are always on different contacts, whichever way you turn it.

63
00:09:00,343 --> 00:09:07,602
Check with the meter. The diagonal legs must be open when released and closed when pressed.

64
00:09:07,602 --> 00:09:17,280
Gate G13: measure every resistor before you insert it. Colour bands are easy to misread.

65
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:27,788
Gate G11: confirm the buzzer is active, rated five volts, takes thirty milliamps or less, and find its plus leg.

66
00:09:27,788 --> 00:09:46,900
Gate G10: the transistor is blocked. Different transistors have different leg orders. For example, the PN2222A is emitter, base, collector, but the BC547 is collector, base, emitter.

67
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:57,993
Read your transistor's marking and look up its own datasheet. Until that is recorded, the transistor connections stay blocked.

68
00:09:57,993 --> 00:10:02,582
Before wiring, record or photograph these items.

69
00:10:02,582 --> 00:10:16,179
The ESP32 shield marking and headers, both sides of each transceiver, the onboard terminator reading, the OLED labels, the transistor part number and the buzzer details.

70
00:10:16,179 --> 00:10:21,783
The transistor item is blocked until you supply it.

71
00:10:21,783 --> 00:10:29,941
Node 1 goes on breadboard A. Seat ESP1 across the centre trench in columns 1 to 15.

72
00:10:29,941 --> 00:10:39,879
Gate G3: every pin must still have at least one free hole beside it on both sides. If not, do not force it.

73
00:10:39,879 --> 00:10:46,428
Place TRX1 in the lower half, around columns 18 to 24.

74
00:10:46,428 --> 00:10:50,867
Everything stays unplugged.

75
00:10:50,867 --> 00:10:58,091
Node 1 uses only four ESP32 pins, all on the 3V3-side header.

76
00:10:58,091 --> 00:11:08,064
Three volt three, ground, RX2 which is GPIO sixteen, and TX2 which is GPIO seventeen.

77
00:11:08,064 --> 00:11:12,543
Every other pin stays empty.

78
00:11:12,543 --> 00:11:16,213
Connection N1-01.

79
00:11:16,213 --> 00:11:18,692
Take a red jumper.

80
00:11:18,692 --> 00:11:27,445
One end goes to ESP1 pin 3V3: the first pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

81
00:11:27,445 --> 00:11:32,739
The other end goes to the TRX1 pin printed 3V3.

82
00:11:32,739 --> 00:11:37,328
Signal: 3V3_N1.

83
00:11:37,328 --> 00:11:47,541
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G1, G2 and G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

84
00:11:47,541 --> 00:12:01,319
Check: Power off: continuity ESP1 3V3 pin to TRX1 3V3 pin; no continuity to any GND or 5 V point.

85
00:12:01,319 --> 00:12:04,963
Connection N1-02.

86
00:12:04,963 --> 00:12:07,547
Take a black jumper.

87
00:12:07,547 --> 00:12:15,856
One end goes to ESP1 pin GND: the second pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

88
00:12:15,856 --> 00:12:20,700
The other end goes to the TRX1 pin printed GND.

89
00:12:20,700 --> 00:12:23,644
Signal: GND.

90
00:12:23,644 --> 00:12:32,967
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G2 and G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

91
00:12:32,967 --> 00:12:40,381
Check: Continuity ESP1 GND to TRX1 GND.

92
00:12:40,381 --> 00:12:44,110
Connection N1-03.

93
00:12:44,110 --> 00:12:46,614
Take a white jumper.

94
00:12:46,614 --> 00:12:57,522
One end goes to ESP1 pin TX2, GPIO 17: the seventh pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

95
00:12:57,522 --> 00:13:02,891
The other end goes to the TRX1 pin printed CTX.

96
00:13:02,891 --> 00:13:08,725
Signal: CAN_TX_N1 (GPIO17).

97
00:13:08,725 --> 00:13:18,938
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G1, G2 and G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

98
00:13:18,938 --> 00:13:27,677
Check: Continuity TX2 pin to CTX pin; NOT to CRX.

99
00:13:27,677 --> 00:13:31,401
Connection N1-04.

100
00:13:31,401 --> 00:13:33,985
Take a brown jumper.

101
00:13:33,985 --> 00:13:44,609
One end goes to ESP1 pin RX2, GPIO 16: the sixth pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

102
00:13:44,609 --> 00:13:49,898
The other end goes to the TRX1 pin printed CRX.

103
00:13:49,898 --> 00:13:55,486
Signal: CAN_RX_N1 (GPIO16).

104
00:13:55,486 --> 00:14:05,699
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G1, G2 and G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

105
00:14:05,699 --> 00:14:14,318
Check: Continuity RX2 pin to CRX pin; NOT to CTX.

106
00:14:14,318 --> 00:14:21,372
Node 1 is wired. Compare your board with this diagram, wire by wire.

107
00:14:21,372 --> 00:14:29,965
With the meter in continuity mode, check each wire end to end, and check that transmit and receive are not swapped.

108
00:14:29,965 --> 00:14:36,464
The terminator and bus wires come later, in the bus scene.

109
00:14:36,464 --> 00:14:42,922
Node two repeats Node one exactly: the same four pins and the same colours.

110
00:14:42,922 --> 00:14:51,761
Node 2 goes on breadboard A. Seat ESP2 across the centre trench in columns 32 to 46.

111
00:14:51,761 --> 00:15:01,699
Gate G3: every pin must still have at least one free hole beside it on both sides. If not, do not force it.

112
00:15:01,699 --> 00:15:08,513
Place TRX2 in the lower half, around columns 49 to 55.

113
00:15:08,513 --> 00:15:11,552
Everything stays unplugged.

114
00:15:11,552 --> 00:15:23,780
What is different: Node two is in the middle of the bus, so it has no terminator, and its 3V3 must never touch Node one's 3V3.

115
00:15:23,780 --> 00:15:31,078
Node 2 uses only four ESP32 pins, all on the 3V3-side header.

116
00:15:31,078 --> 00:15:41,052
Three volt three, ground, RX2 which is GPIO sixteen, and TX2 which is GPIO seventeen.

117
00:15:41,052 --> 00:15:45,531
Every other pin stays empty.

118
00:15:45,531 --> 00:15:49,185
Connection N2-01.

119
00:15:49,185 --> 00:15:51,664
Take a red jumper.

120
00:15:51,664 --> 00:16:00,453
One end goes to ESP2 pin 3V3: the first pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

121
00:16:00,453 --> 00:16:05,746
The other end goes to the TRX2 pin printed 3V3.

122
00:16:05,746 --> 00:16:10,301
Signal: 3V3_N2.

123
00:16:10,301 --> 00:16:20,513
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G1, G2 and G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

124
00:16:20,513 --> 00:16:32,587
Check: Continuity ESP2 3V3 to TRX2 3V3. NO continuity to Node 1 3V3.

125
00:16:32,587 --> 00:16:36,216
Connection N2-02.

126
00:16:36,216 --> 00:16:38,800
Take a black jumper.

127
00:16:38,800 --> 00:16:47,144
One end goes to ESP2 pin GND: the second pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

128
00:16:47,144 --> 00:16:51,987
The other end goes to the TRX2 pin printed GND.

129
00:16:51,987 --> 00:16:54,932
Signal: GND.

130
00:16:54,932 --> 00:17:04,255
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G2 and G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

131
00:17:04,255 --> 00:17:11,723
Check: Continuity ESP2 GND to TRX2 GND.

132
00:17:11,723 --> 00:17:15,438
Connection N2-03.

133
00:17:15,438 --> 00:17:17,942
Take a white jumper.

134
00:17:17,942 --> 00:17:28,900
One end goes to ESP2 pin TX2, GPIO 17: the seventh pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

135
00:17:28,900 --> 00:17:34,269
The other end goes to the TRX2 pin printed CTX.

136
00:17:34,269 --> 00:17:40,093
Signal: CAN_TX_N2 (GPIO17).

137
00:17:40,093 --> 00:17:50,306
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G1, G2 and G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

138
00:17:50,306 --> 00:17:59,044
Check: Continuity TX2 pin to CTX pin; NOT to CRX.

139
00:17:59,044 --> 00:18:02,759
Connection N2-04.

140
00:18:02,759 --> 00:18:05,343
Take a brown jumper.

141
00:18:05,343 --> 00:18:16,001
One end goes to ESP2 pin RX2, GPIO 16: the sixth pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

142
00:18:16,001 --> 00:18:21,300
The other end goes to the TRX2 pin printed CRX.

143
00:18:21,300 --> 00:18:26,874
Signal: CAN_RX_N2 (GPIO16).

144
00:18:26,874 --> 00:18:37,087
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G1, G2 and G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

145
00:18:37,087 --> 00:18:45,706
Check: Continuity RX2 pin to CRX pin; NOT to CTX.

146
00:18:45,706 --> 00:18:52,779
Node 2 is wired. Compare your board with this diagram, wire by wire.

147
00:18:52,779 --> 00:19:01,373
With the meter in continuity mode, check each wire end to end, and check that transmit and receive are not swapped.

148
00:19:01,373 --> 00:19:07,871
The terminator and bus wires come later, in the bus scene.

149
00:19:07,871 --> 00:19:15,725
Node three has breadboard B to itself. Seat ESP3 in columns one to fifteen.

150
00:19:15,725 --> 00:19:23,968
Put TRX3 and the OLED in the lower half, the buzzer driver, LEDs and buttons in the upper half.

151
00:19:23,968 --> 00:19:32,801
Gate G14: check each rail with the meter from end to end. Some breadboards split their rails in the middle.

152
00:19:32,801 --> 00:19:43,190
On breadboard B, the bottom plus rail is 3V3 for Node three only. Both minus rails are ground.

153
00:19:43,190 --> 00:19:46,914
Connection N3-01.

154
00:19:46,914 --> 00:19:49,393
Take a red jumper.

155
00:19:49,393 --> 00:19:58,196
One end goes to ESP3 pin 3V3: the first pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

156
00:19:58,196 --> 00:20:05,375
The other end goes to the bottom plus rail of breadboard B, which is 3V3 for Node 3.

157
00:20:05,375 --> 00:20:09,999
Signal: 3V3_N3.

158
00:20:09,999 --> 00:20:19,577
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G2 and G14 have passed and you have recorded the results.

159
00:20:19,577 --> 00:20:26,200
Check: Continuity 3V3 pin to bottom + rail.

160
00:20:26,200 --> 00:20:29,900
Connection N3-02.

161
00:20:29,900 --> 00:20:32,484
Take a black jumper.

162
00:20:32,484 --> 00:20:40,847
One end goes to ESP3 pin GND: the second pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

163
00:20:40,847 --> 00:20:46,836
The other end goes to the bottom minus rail of breadboard B, which is ground.

164
00:20:46,836 --> 00:20:49,780
Signal: GND.

165
00:20:49,780 --> 00:20:59,358
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G2 and G14 have passed and you have recorded the results.

166
00:20:59,358 --> 00:21:08,107
Check: Continuity GND pin (3V3 side) to bottom - rail.

167
00:21:08,107 --> 00:21:11,891
Connection N3-03.

168
00:21:11,891 --> 00:21:14,475
Take a black jumper.

169
00:21:14,475 --> 00:21:20,284
One end goes to the bottom minus rail of breadboard B, which is ground.

170
00:21:20,284 --> 00:21:26,273
The other end goes to the top minus rail of breadboard B, which is ground.

171
00:21:26,273 --> 00:21:32,306
Signal: GND (rail link, far right end of board B).

172
00:21:32,306 --> 00:21:41,119
Status: provisional. Make it only after gate G14 have passed and you have recorded the results.

173
00:21:41,119 --> 00:21:53,088
Check: Continuity bottom - rail to top - rail: near 0 Ohm. Never link a - rail to a + rail.

174
00:21:53,088 --> 00:21:56,867
Connection N3-04.

175
00:21:56,867 --> 00:21:59,346
Take a red jumper.

176
00:21:59,346 --> 00:22:06,335
One end goes to the bottom plus rail of breadboard B, which is 3V3 for Node 3.

177
00:22:06,335 --> 00:22:11,633
The other end goes to the TRX3 pin printed 3V3.

178
00:22:11,633 --> 00:22:16,257
Signal: 3V3_N3.

179
00:22:16,257 --> 00:22:24,810
Status: provisional. Make it only after gate G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

180
00:22:24,810 --> 00:22:31,944
Check: Continuity + rail to TRX3 3V3.

181
00:22:31,944 --> 00:22:35,768
Connection N3-05.

182
00:22:35,768 --> 00:22:38,353
Take a black jumper.

183
00:22:38,353 --> 00:22:44,161
One end goes to the bottom minus rail of breadboard B, which is ground.

184
00:22:44,161 --> 00:22:49,015
The other end goes to the TRX3 pin printed GND.

185
00:22:49,015 --> 00:22:51,959
Signal: GND.

186
00:22:51,959 --> 00:23:00,512
Status: provisional. Make it only after gate G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

187
00:23:00,512 --> 00:23:07,386
Check: Continuity - rail to TRX3 GND.

188
00:23:07,386 --> 00:23:11,311
Connection N3-06.

189
00:23:11,311 --> 00:23:13,815
Take a white jumper.

190
00:23:13,815 --> 00:23:24,788
One end goes to ESP3 pin TX2, GPIO 17: the seventh pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

191
00:23:24,788 --> 00:23:30,162
The other end goes to the TRX3 pin printed CTX.

192
00:23:30,162 --> 00:23:36,061
Signal: CAN_TX_N3 (GPIO17).

193
00:23:36,061 --> 00:23:46,274
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G1, G2 and G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

194
00:23:46,274 --> 00:23:54,532
Check: Continuity TX2 to CTX; NOT to CRX.

195
00:23:54,532 --> 00:23:58,372
Connection N3-07.

196
00:23:58,372 --> 00:24:00,956
Take a brown jumper.

197
00:24:00,956 --> 00:24:11,624
One end goes to ESP3 pin RX2, GPIO 16: the sixth pin from the USB end on the 3V3 side header.

198
00:24:11,624 --> 00:24:16,923
The other end goes to the TRX3 pin printed CRX.

199
00:24:16,923 --> 00:24:22,572
Signal: CAN_RX_N3 (GPIO16).

200
00:24:22,572 --> 00:24:32,785
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G1, G2 and G4 have passed and you have recorded the results.

201
00:24:32,785 --> 00:24:40,944
Check: Continuity RX2 to CRX; NOT to CTX.

202
00:24:40,944 --> 00:24:47,887
Now the OLED. Its pins are wired by printed name, not by position.

203
00:24:47,887 --> 00:25:00,500
SDA goes to GPIO twenty-one and SCL to GPIO twenty-two. These are the standard I2C pins in the Arduino ESP32 core.

204
00:25:00,500 --> 00:25:07,804
That is also why Node three does not use twenty-one and twenty-two for CAN.

