328
00:37:07,442 --> 00:37:15,905
Status: provisional. Make it only after gate G8 have passed and you have recorded the results.

329
00:37:15,905 --> 00:37:24,054
Check: D33-to-GND: open released, ~0 Ohm pressed.

330
00:37:24,054 --> 00:37:34,422
The buzzer needs five volts, so a GPIO cannot drive it directly. A small NPN transistor switches its low side.

331
00:37:34,422 --> 00:37:44,065
GPIO thirteen drives the base through one kilo-ohm, about two point six milliamps, enough to switch thirty milliamps.

332
00:37:44,065 --> 00:37:51,523
A ten kilo-ohm resistor from base to ground keeps the buzzer quiet while the ESP32 boots.

333
00:37:51,523 --> 00:38:03,041
The buzzer's plus side takes five volts from Node three's own VIN pin, later. Its minus side goes to the collector, and the emitter goes to ground.

334
00:38:03,041 --> 00:38:11,374
The transistor's leg order is still blocked, so the transistor connections are not made now.

335
00:38:11,374 --> 00:38:15,053
Connection N3-27.

336
00:38:15,053 --> 00:38:17,608
Take a grey jumper.

337
00:38:17,608 --> 00:38:27,721
One end goes to ESP3 pin D13, GPIO 13: the third pin from the USB end on the VIN side header.

338
00:38:27,721 --> 00:38:34,045
The other end goes to R_B, the 1 kOhm base resistor, end 1.

339
00:38:34,045 --> 00:38:39,364
Signal: BUZZER_DRIVE (GPIO13).

340
00:38:39,364 --> 00:38:48,891
Status: provisional. Make it only after gates G2 and G13 have passed and you have recorded the results.

341
00:38:48,891 --> 00:39:09,293
Check: Continuity D13 (3rd pin from USB, VIN side) to one end of the 1 kOhm. The 4th pin, D12, is a strapping pin: make sure it stays EMPTY. D13 has NO path to the buzzer except through Q1.

342
00:39:09,293 --> 00:39:21,421
Double check: pin D13 is the third pin from USB. The fourth pin, D12, is a strapping pin and must stay empty.

343
00:39:21,421 --> 00:39:24,931
